---
import GuidePage from '../../layouts/GuidePage.astro';
import Chip from '../../components/Chip.astro';
import Callout from '../../components/Callout.astro';
import GoDeeper from '../../components/GoDeeper.astro';
import Diagram from '../../components/Diagram.astro';
import CodeCaption from '../../components/CodeCaption.astro';
import Snippet from '../../components/Snippet.astro';
---

<GuidePage title='Component Breakdown - App Router Guide' root='..'>
  <div class='kicker'>Chapter 17 · Part C: Decouple the Parts</div>
  <h1>Component Breakdown</h1>
  <p class='lede'>
    Every chapter so far has quietly been about where to draw component lines. This one makes it the
    subject: how a server-first mentality changes the way you break a feature into components, why
    the splits feel awkward at first, the patterns to reach for, and why one component per file is
    what holds the whole scheme together.
  </p>

  <Callout kind='warn' title='This changes how you break things apart'>
    <p>
      Read this chapter as a warning as much as a guide. The App Router and React 19 <strong
        >fundamentally change how a UI gets broken into components</strong
      >: where the boundary lines are drawn, where each component lives, how a feature is split up,
      how the pieces are imported, and what each piece is allowed to depend on. Every one of those
      decisions now carries architectural weight: it determines what can render on the server, what
      can stream, what can be cached, and what ships to the browser. Which means <strong
        >when and how something is treated as a component is a highly technical design question</strong
      >, not a visual or UX one. "It looks like one thing in the mock" has never been a weaker
      argument for making it one component than it is in this stack.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2 id='axes'>17.1 The axes of a split have changed</h2>
  <p>
    Traditional React taught one decomposition instinct: split by <em>visual shape and reuse</em>. A
    card here, a toolbar there, extract when you see it twice. Those considerations still exist, but
    server-first demotes them, because three harder axes now come first:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>The boundary axis: server or client.</strong> A component either stays on the server (renders
      to HTML, ships no JS, may be <code>async</code>) or crosses to the client (hydrates,
      interacts, costs bundle). One component cannot be both, so anything that mixes "renders known
      content" with "handles interaction" is two components waiting to be separated (<a
        href='05-client-server-boundaries.html'>§5</a
      >).
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The data axis: one component per independent question.</strong> Each independent data need
      becomes its own <code>async</code> server component, because the component is the unit that can
      await, suspend, and stream on its own (<a href='14-blocking-requests.html'>§14</a>,
      <a href='15-minimize-the-wires.html'>§15</a>). Splitting by data is what makes parallelism and
      streaming fall out for free.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The containment axis: frame or content.</strong> The known frame renders immediately and
      reserves space; the unknown content suspends inside it. If one component renders both, the frame
      is hostage to the content's data (<a href='10-containers-content-shifting.html'>§10</a>,
      <a href='11-suspense-streaming.html'>§11</a>).
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    This is why server-first decomposition feels <strong>awkward at first</strong>. The old instinct
    says a program card is obviously one component: it is one visual thing. The new axes say it is
    at least three: a frame that is known, a stats region that awaits data, and a menu that
    interacts. Cutting along those seams feels like over-fragmenting until you notice what each
    fragment is: exactly one capability. One frames, one fetches, one interacts. The seams follow <em
      >capabilities</em
    >, not the visual outline, and every capability can then be moved, cached, streamed, or shipped
    (or not shipped) independently.
  </p>

  <Callout kind='voice' title='The instinct to retrain'>
    <p>
      "Is this one visual thing?" is no longer the question. The questions are: does any part of
      this wait for data the rest doesn't? Does any part of this interact while the rest just
      renders? If either answer is yes, the component line goes <em>there</em>, even when the mock
      shows a single seamless card.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2 id='worked'>17.2 The same card, broken both ways</h2>
  <p>
    Here is the old-world version: one file, one component, visually faithful to the mock, and wrong
    on all three axes.
  </p>

  <Snippet>
    <pre
      class='code v-bad'
      is:raw><span class="filename">ProgramCard.tsx - one component, three capabilities welded together</span><span class="tok-dir">"use client"</span>;
<span class="tok-kw">export function</span> <span class="tok-fn">ProgramCard</span>({ programId }: { programId: number }) {
  <span class="tok-kw">const</span> { data, isLoading } = <span class="tok-fn">useProgramStats</span>(programId);
  <span class="tok-kw">const</span> [menuOpen, setMenuOpen] = <span class="tok-fn">useState</span>(<span class="tok-kw">false</span>);

  <span class="tok-kw">if</span> (isLoading) <span class="tok-kw">return</span> &lt;<span class="tok-fn">CardSkeleton</span> /&gt;;

  <span class="tok-kw">return</span> (
    &lt;section <span class="tok-prop">className</span>=<span class="tok-str">"card"</span>&gt;
      &lt;header&gt;
        &lt;h3&gt;Program&lt;/h3&gt;
        &lt;<span class="tok-fn">Menu</span> <span class="tok-prop">open</span>={menuOpen} <span class="tok-prop">onToggle</span>={setMenuOpen}&gt;…&lt;/<span class="tok-fn">Menu</span>&gt;
      &lt;/header&gt;
      &lt;<span class="tok-fn">StatRow</span> <span class="tok-prop">stats</span>={data} /&gt;
    &lt;/section&gt;
  );
}</pre>
    <CodeCaption>
      Because the menu needs a click, the whole card is client code; because the stats need data,
      the whole card (frame included) hides behind <code>isLoading</code>; and because it is one
      component, none of it can stream, cache, or ship independently. The header that required
      nothing waits for everything.
    </CodeCaption>
  </Snippet>

  <p>Cut along the capability seams instead and the card becomes a small folder:</p>

  <Snippet>
    <pre
      class='code v-good'
      is:raw><span class="filename">components/programs/card/ - one capability per file</span>card/
├── ProgramCard.tsx               <span class="tok-com">server: the known frame</span>
├── ProgramCardStats.tsx          <span class="tok-com">server, async: one data question</span>
├── ProgramCardStatsSkeleton.tsx  <span class="tok-com">the fallback, colocated (§11)</span>
├── ProgramCardMenu.tsx           <span class="tok-com">client: the trigger, the only first-paint JS</span>
└── ProgramCardMenuPanel.tsx      <span class="tok-com">client: the floating layer, loaded on demand</span></pre>
  </Snippet>

  <Snippet>
    <pre
      class='code v-good'
      is:raw><span class="filename">ProgramCard.tsx - server: frame renders immediately, reserves space</span><span class="tok-kw">export function</span> <span class="tok-fn">ProgramCard</span>({ programId }: { programId: number }) {
  <span class="tok-kw">return</span> (
    &lt;section <span class="tok-prop">className</span>=<span class="tok-str">"card"</span>&gt;
      &lt;header <span class="tok-prop">className</span>=<span class="tok-str">"flex items-center justify-between"</span>&gt;
        &lt;h3&gt;Program&lt;/h3&gt;
        &lt;<span class="tok-fn">ProgramCardMenu</span> <span class="tok-prop">programId</span>={programId} /&gt;
      &lt;/header&gt;
      &lt;div <span class="tok-prop">className</span>=<span class="tok-str">"h-24"</span>&gt;
        &lt;<span class="tok-fn">Suspense</span> <span class="tok-prop">fallback</span>={&lt;<span class="tok-fn">ProgramCardStatsSkeleton</span> /&gt;}&gt;
          &lt;<span class="tok-fn">ProgramCardStats</span> <span class="tok-prop">programId</span>={programId} /&gt;
        &lt;/<span class="tok-fn">Suspense</span>&gt;
      &lt;/div&gt;
    &lt;/section&gt;
  );
}</pre>
    <CodeCaption>
      The frame is a server component with no data and no state: it paints on the first byte,
      reserves the stats region's height, and composes the other capabilities without owning them.
    </CodeCaption>
  </Snippet>

  <Snippet>
    <pre
      class='code v-good'
      is:raw><span class="filename">ProgramCardStats.tsx - server, async: exactly one question</span><span class="tok-kw">export async function</span> <span class="tok-fn">ProgramCardStats</span>({ programId }: { programId: number }) {
  <span class="tok-com">// React.cache'd (§16): duplicate askers collapse into one execution.</span>
  <span class="tok-kw">const</span> stats = <span class="tok-kw">await</span> <span class="tok-fn">getProgramStats</span>(programId);
  <span class="tok-kw">return</span> &lt;<span class="tok-fn">StatRow</span> <span class="tok-prop">stats</span>={stats} /&gt;;
}</pre>
    <CodeCaption>
      The whole file is one await and one render. That smallness is not tidiness for its own sake:
      it is what lets this region suspend and stream alone while the frame and the menu never wait.
    </CodeCaption>
  </Snippet>

  <Snippet>
    <pre
      class='code v-good'
      is:raw><span class="filename">ProgramCardMenu.tsx - client: the trigger is the card's entire first-paint cost</span><span class="tok-dir">"use client"</span>;
<span class="tok-kw">import</span> dynamic <span class="tok-kw">from</span> <span class="tok-str">"next/dynamic"</span>;

<span class="tok-kw">const</span> ProgramCardMenuPanel = <span class="tok-fn">dynamic</span>(() =&gt;
  <span class="tok-kw">import</span>(<span class="tok-str">"./ProgramCardMenuPanel"</span>).<span class="tok-fn">then</span>((m) =&gt; m.ProgramCardMenuPanel)
);

<span class="tok-kw">export function</span> <span class="tok-fn">ProgramCardMenu</span>({ programId }: { programId: number }) {
  <span class="tok-kw">const</span> [open, setOpen] = <span class="tok-fn">useState</span>(<span class="tok-kw">false</span>);
  <span class="tok-kw">return</span> (
    &lt;&gt;
      &lt;<span class="tok-fn">IconButton</span> <span class="tok-prop">aria-label</span>=<span class="tok-str">"Program actions"</span> <span class="tok-prop">onClick</span>={() =&gt; <span class="tok-fn">setOpen</span>((o) =&gt; !o)} /&gt;
      <span class="tok-com">{/* Nothing below exists - as DOM or as JS - until the first click. */}</span>
      {open &amp;&amp; &lt;<span class="tok-fn">ProgramCardMenuPanel</span> <span class="tok-prop">programId</span>={programId} <span class="tok-prop">onClose</span>={() =&gt; <span class="tok-fn">setOpen</span>(<span class="tok-kw">false</span>)} /&gt;}
    &lt;/&gt;
  );
}</pre>
    <CodeCaption>
      The menu is a floating element that is closed by default, which makes it the purest case of
      the <a href='08-bundle-size-code-splitting.html'>§8</a> test: it is not needed on first render.
      So it is deferred twice. The conditional mount defers its <em>render</em> until the click, and <code
        >dynamic</code
      > (<a href='08-bundle-size-code-splitting.html#heavyweight'>§8.3</a>) defers its <em
        >download</em
      > until the same click. The trigger button above is the only code in the card's first-paint bundle.
      The general trigger/panel split, including how to keep it under a UI library's compound API, is
      <a href='08-bundle-size-code-splitting.html#triggers'>§8.4</a>.
    </CodeCaption>
  </Snippet>

  <Snippet>
    <pre
      class='code v-good'
      is:raw><span class="filename">ProgramCardMenuPanel.tsx - client: the floating layer, mounted and downloaded on demand</span><span class="tok-dir">"use client"</span>;
<span class="tok-kw">export function</span> <span class="tok-fn">ProgramCardMenuPanel</span>({
  programId,
  onClose,
}: {
  programId: number;
  onClose: () =&gt; void;
}) {
  <span class="tok-kw">const</span> [isPending, startTransition] = <span class="tok-fn">useTransition</span>();
  <span class="tok-kw">return</span> (
    &lt;<span class="tok-fn">Popover</span> <span class="tok-prop">onClose</span>={onClose}&gt;
      &lt;<span class="tok-fn">MenuItem</span>
        <span class="tok-prop">disabled</span>={isPending}
        <span class="tok-prop">onSelect</span>={() =&gt;
          <span class="tok-fn">startTransition</span>(<span class="tok-kw">async</span> () =&gt; {
            <span class="tok-kw">const</span> { error } = <span class="tok-kw">await</span> <span class="tok-fn">archiveProgram</span>(programId);
            <span class="tok-kw">if</span> (error) toast.<span class="tok-fn">error</span>(error);
            <span class="tok-kw">else</span> <span class="tok-fn">onClose</span>();
          })
        }
      &gt;
        Archive
      &lt;/<span class="tok-fn">MenuItem</span>&gt;
    &lt;/<span class="tok-fn">Popover</span>&gt;
  );
}</pre>
    <CodeCaption>
      Still the self-contained leaf of
      <a href='05-client-server-boundaries.html#self-contained'>§5.7</a>: it knows a name (<code
        >programId</code
      >), fires an action, and holds zero references to its surroundings. Splitting it from its
      trigger is not ceremony. This file is a separate chunk that most visitors never download,
      because most visits never open the menu.
    </CodeCaption>
  </Snippet>

  <p>
    Count what the "awkward" split bought: the frame paints immediately (<a href='04-ssr.html'>§4</a
    >), the stats stream into reserved space without moving anything (<a
      href='10-containers-content-shifting.html'>§10</a
    >,
    <a href='11-suspense-streaming.html'>§11</a>), the menu trigger is the entire first-paint bundle
    cost and the panel behind it downloads only for users who open it (<a
      href='08-bundle-size-code-splitting.html'>§8</a
    >), the data question dedupes with every other asker (<a href='16-dedupe-caching.html'>§16</a>),
    and all five files are independently portable (<a href='15-minimize-the-wires.html#portability'
      >§15.5</a
    >). None of that is achievable while the card is one component, because every one of those
    behaviors attaches to a component boundary.
  </p>

  <h2 id='catalog'>17.3 The pattern catalog</h2>
  <p>
    The recurring shapes, in one place. Each is defined in depth in its home chapter; this table is
    the index you skim while decomposing a feature.
  </p>
  <table class='tbl'>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Pattern</th>
        <th>Job</th>
        <th>Home</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Known frame</strong></td>
        <td>
          Server component that renders chrome and reserves space; contains the unknown, never waits
          for it.
        </td>
        <td><a href='10-containers-content-shifting.html'>§10</a></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Async region</strong></td>
        <td>
          <code>async</code> server component owning exactly one data question; the unit of suspending
          and streaming.
        </td>
        <td>
          <a href='11-suspense-streaming.html'>§11</a>,
          <a href='14-blocking-requests.html'>§14</a>
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Colocated skeleton</strong></td>
        <td> The async region's fallback, next to the component whose shape it mirrors. </td>
        <td><a href='11-suspense-streaming.html#skeletons'>§11.4</a></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Interaction leaf</strong></td>
        <td>
          Smallest client component containing the interaction atom; self-contained via names (key,
          tag, param), not wires.
        </td>
        <td>
          <a href='05-client-server-boundaries.html#push-down'>§5.2</a>,
          <a href='05-client-server-boundaries.html#self-contained'>§5.7</a>
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Client shell with slots</strong></td>
        <td>
          Client wrapper owning one interaction (open, drag, select); server content passes through
          JSX-valued props.
        </td>
        <td>
          <a href='05-client-server-boundaries.html#children-pattern'>§5.4</a>,
          <a href='15-minimize-the-wires.html#portability'>§15.5</a>
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Thin provider</strong></td>
        <td>
          Context provider as a client wrapper around <code>&#123;children&#125;</code>, rendered as
          deep as possible; converts nothing below it.
        </td>
        <td><a href='05-client-server-boundaries.html#children-pattern'>§5.4</a></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Wrapped third party</strong></td>
        <td>
          One-line <code>"use client"</code> re-export that gives an undeclared library a boundary and
          your import graph a seam.
        </td>
        <td><a href='05-client-server-boundaries.html#third-party'>§5.5</a></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Lazy heavyweight</strong></td>
        <td>
          Client component loaded on interaction via <code>dynamic()</code>; charts, editors,
          anything that shouldn't tax first render.
        </td>
        <td><a href='08-bundle-size-code-splitting.html'>§8</a></td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2 id='sequence'>17.4 The decomposition sequence</h2>
  <p>Applied in order, the axes become a repeatable procedure for any page or feature:</p>

