  <h2 id='one-per-file'>17.5 One component per file</h2>
  <p>
    This habit sounds like style guidance. In the App Router it is structural, because
    <strong
      >the module, not the component, is the unit that crosses the server/client boundary.</strong
    >
    <Chip k='react' />
    <Chip k='critical' />
    <code>"use client"</code> marks a
    <em>file</em>; every export in it, and everything that file imports, crosses together (<a
      href='05-client-server-boundaries.html#what-it-means'>§5.1</a
    >). The moment a file holds two components, their fates are welded: a helper component sharing a
    file with an interactive one becomes client code it never asked to be; a server-able piece of UI
    stuck in a client file drags its imports into the bundle (<a
      href='08-bundle-size-code-splitting.html'>§8</a
    >). One component per file means the import graph <em>is</em> the boundary map: you can read where
    the client zone starts from the filesystem.
  </p>
  <p>
    It also keeps every decomposition decision cheap to revise. Splitting a stats region out of a
    card, moving a leaf across the boundary, lazy-loading a heavyweight, swapping a shell: each is a
    file operation when components live alone, and an untangling when they don't. The
    awkward-at-first splits of
    <a href='#axes'>§17.1</a> stay painless only under this rule; it is the difference between "decomposition
    is how the code gets written" and "decomposition is a refactor to schedule." The guide's own examples
    practice it everywhere: the sidebar split in
    <a href='05-client-server-boundaries.html#library-leaves'>§5.5</a>, the SWR pair in
    <a href='05-client-server-boundaries.html#swr-refresh'>§5.8</a>, the card above.
  </p>

  <Callout kind='info' title='Corollaries'>
    <p>
      Skeletons live in their own files next to the component they mirror (importable by the frame
      without touching the async region). Barrel files that re-export server and client components
      together undo the legibility, and a barrel that pulls in a client module can widen what ships
      (<a href='08-bundle-size-code-splitting.html'>§8</a>); import from the file, not the folder.
      And a file that suddenly wants a second export is usually a component announcing it has grown
      a second capability: split it.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2 id='rules'>The rules</h2>
  <table class='tbl'>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Rule</th>
        <th>Scope</th>
        <th>Importance</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td>
          Components split along <strong>capability seams</strong> (boundary, data, containment) before
          visual or reuse instincts get a vote.
        </td>
        <td><Chip k='react' /></td>
        <td><Chip k='critical' /></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>
          Anything that mixes "renders known content" with "waits for data" or "handles interaction"
          is <strong>two components waiting to be separated</strong>.
        </td>
        <td><Chip k='react' /></td>
        <td><Chip k='critical' /></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>
          <strong>One component per file.</strong> The module is what crosses the boundary; the filesystem
          should read as the boundary map.
        </td>
        <td><Chip k='react' /></td>
        <td><Chip k='critical' /></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>
          Skeletons are <strong>colocated, separate files</strong>; frames import them without
          touching the async region they stand in for.
        </td>
        <td><Chip k='next' /></td>
        <td><Chip k='very' /></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>
          Decompose in sequence: <strong>frame → data → containment → interactivity</strong>, then
          keep every split one file.
        </td>
        <td><Chip k='react' /></td>
        <td><Chip k='very' /></td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

